  "contracts": {
    "lem-tdmath-extended-convex-epigraph-equivalence": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-tdmath-extended-convex-function",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Fix an integer $d\\ge1$. Write $\\overline{\\mathbb R}=\\mathbb R\\cup\\{+\\infty\\}$ with its usual order and extended addition; in a convex combination set $0\\cdot(+\\infty)=0$ and $\\lambda\\cdot(+\\infty)=+\\infty$ for $\\lambda>0$. A function $f:\\mathbb R^d\\to\\overline{\\mathbb R}$ is **proper** when it never takes the value $-\\infty$ and its effective domain\n\n$$\\operatorname{dom}f:=\\{x\\in\\mathbb R^d:f(x)<+\\infty\\}$$\n\nis nonempty. It is **convex** when\n\n$$f((1-t)x+ty)\\le (1-t)f(x)+tf(y)\\qquad(x,y\\in\\mathbb R^d,\\ t\\in[0,1]).$$\n\nIt is **lower semicontinuous** when each weak sublevel set $\\{x:f(x)\\le a\\}$ is closed in the Euclidean topology, for every $a\\in\\mathbb R$. Its **epigraph** is\n\n$$\\operatorname{epi}f:=\\{(x,r)\\in\\mathbb R^d\\times\\mathbb R:f(x)\\le r\\}.$$\n\nOn this page “closed proper convex” means proper, convex, and lower semicontinuous in these conventions. The pairing of $p,x\\in\\mathbb R^d$ is the Euclidean inner product $\\langle p,x\\rangle$ of [[def-euclidean-inner-product]].",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "1.3",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-metric-topology",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(X,d)$ be a metric space ([[def-metric-space]]).\n\nA subset $U \\subseteq X$ is **open in $(X,d)$** if for every $x \\in U$ there is a\nreal $r > 0$ with $B(x,r) \\subseteq U$ ([[def-metric-ball]]). A subset\n$F \\subseteq X$ is **closed in $(X,d)$** if its complement $X \\setminus F$ is\nopen.\n\nThe collection\n\n$$\\mathcal{T}_d := \\{\\, U \\subseteq X : U \\text{ is open in } (X,d) \\,\\}$$\n\nof all open subsets is the **metric topology** of $d$ on $X$. A subset of $X$\nthat is both open and closed is called **clopen**.\n\n**Two sets are open for trivial reasons.** $\\emptyset$ is open, because the\ndefining condition quantifies over no points; and $X$ is open, because\n$B(x,r) \\subseteq X$ for every $x$ and every $r > 0$. Consequently $X$ and\n$\\emptyset$ are also closed, and both are clopen.\n\n**A neighbourhood of a point $x$** is any set containing an open set that\ncontains $x$; an **open neighbourhood** is an open set containing $x$. In a\nmetric space, a set is a neighbourhood of $x$ exactly when it contains some\nball $B(x,r)$ with $r>0$. Thus $U$ is open exactly when it is a neighbourhood of\neach of its points, and metric balls suffice to test this condition.\n\n**The metric, not the set, determines $\\mathcal{T}_d$.** Two metrics on the same\nset may have different metric topologies, and two different metrics may have the\nsame one; the systematic comparison is [[def-equivalent-metrics]].",
          "uses": [
            "1.3",
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Suppose $f$ is convex and take $(x,r),(y,s)\\in\\operatorname{epi}f$. For $t\\in[0,1]$, the chord inequality gives $f((1-t)x+ty)\\le(1-t)f(x)+tf(y)\\le(1-t)r+ts$, so $(1-t)(x,r)+t(y,s)\\in\\operatorname{epi}f$. Thus the epigraph is convex.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "Conversely, if the epigraph is convex and $f(x),f(y)$ are finite, then $(x,f(x))$ and $(y,f(y))$ lie in it; their convex combination lies in it and gives the chord inequality. If either value is $+\\infty$ and its coefficient is positive, the right side is $+\\infty$ and the inequality is automatic; at $t=0$ or $t=1$ it is equality.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-3",
          "claim": "Suppose $\\operatorname{epi}f$ is closed and fix $a\\in\\mathbb R$. For $n=d,d+1$ write $d_n(u,v)=\\bigl(\\sum_{j=1}^n(u_j-v_j)^2\\bigr)^{1/2}$ for the Euclidean metric. The slice map $i_a(x)=(x,a)$ satisfies $d_{d+1}(i_a(x),i_a(y))=d_d(x,y)$, so it is continuous. If $f(x)>a$, then $(x,a)\\notin\\operatorname{epi}f$; closedness gives $\\varepsilon>0$ such that the $d_{d+1}$-ball of radius $\\varepsilon$ around $(x,a)$ misses $\\operatorname{epi}f$. Whenever $d_d(x,y)<\\varepsilon$, the distance identity puts $(y,a)$ in that ball, so $f(y)>a$. Thus the complement of $\\{y:f(y)\\le a\\}$ is open, and this weak sublevel set is closed. Equivalently, it is the closed-preimage step for $i_a$.",
          "step": "1.3",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "construct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Conversely suppose every weak sublevel set is closed and let $(x,r)\\notin\\operatorname{epi}f$, so $r<f(x)$. Choose a finite real $a$ with $r<a<f(x)$ when $f(x)$ is finite, and choose $a=r+1$ when $f(x)=+\\infty$. The set $N=\\{y:f(y)>a\\}$ is an open neighborhood of $x$, as it is the complement of a closed weak sublevel set. Choose $\\eta>0$ with $r+\\eta<a$. For every $y\\in N$ and $s\\in(r-\\eta,r+\\eta)$ one has $s<a<f(y)$, so $(y,s)\\notin\\operatorname{epi}f$. Thus $N\\times(r-\\eta,r+\\eta)$ is an open neighborhood of $(x,r)$ disjoint from the epigraph. Its complement is open at every point outside the epigraph, so the epigraph is closed.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "construct"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
