        "notes": "Reviewed both steps and the exact conjugate/subgradient definition; Bianchi §2.3 Proposition 2.9 agrees at finite x. The supremum bounds each defining value even when infinite. Equality with the finite value at x is equivalent to all finite-domain upper bounds, and outside the domain the subgradient inequality is automatic. Conversely the attained finite upper bound yields equality, so no convexity, lsc, uniqueness, or unassumed existence is needed. No AC."
