    "thm-tdmath-finite-dimensional-biconjugation": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-tdmath-extended-convex-function",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Fix an integer $d\\ge1$. Write $\\overline{\\mathbb R}=\\mathbb R\\cup\\{+\\infty\\}$ with its usual order and extended addition; in a convex combination set $0\\cdot(+\\infty)=0$ and $\\lambda\\cdot(+\\infty)=+\\infty$ for $\\lambda>0$. A function $f:\\mathbb R^d\\to\\overline{\\mathbb R}$ is **proper** when it never takes the value $-\\infty$ and its effective domain\n\n$$\\operatorname{dom}f:=\\{x\\in\\mathbb R^d:f(x)<+\\infty\\}$$\n\nis nonempty. It is **convex** when\n\n$$f((1-t)x+ty)\\le (1-t)f(x)+tf(y)\\qquad(x,y\\in\\mathbb R^d,\\ t\\in[0,1]).$$\n\nIt is **lower semicontinuous** when each weak sublevel set $\\{x:f(x)\\le a\\}$ is closed in the Euclidean topology, for every $a\\in\\mathbb R$. Its **epigraph** is\n\n$$\\operatorname{epi}f:=\\{(x,r)\\in\\mathbb R^d\\times\\mathbb R:f(x)\\le r\\}.$$\n\nOn this page “closed proper convex” means proper, convex, and lower semicontinuous in these conventions. The pairing of $p,x\\in\\mathbb R^d$ is the Euclidean inner product $\\langle p,x\\rangle$ of [[def-euclidean-inner-product]].",
          "uses": [
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-tdmath-extended-convex-epigraph-equivalence",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $d\\ge1$ and let $f:\\mathbb R^d\\to\\overline{\\mathbb R}$ use the conventions of [[def-tdmath-extended-convex-function]]. Then $f$ is convex if and only if $\\operatorname{epi}f$ is a convex subset of $\\mathbb R^{d+1}$, and $f$ is lower semicontinuous if and only if $\\operatorname{epi}f$ is closed.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "1.3",
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-tdmath-closed-convex-separation",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $n\\ge1$, let $E\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ be nonempty, closed, and convex, and let $z\\notin E$. Then there are a nonzero $u\\in\\mathbb R^n$ and a real $c$ such that\n\n$$\\langle u,e\\rangle\\le c<\\langle u,z\\rangle\\qquad(e\\in E).$$\n\nThus an affine hyperplane strictly separates the point from the entire set.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "1.3",
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "def-tdmath-fenchel-transform-and-subgradient",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $f:\\mathbb R^d\\to\\overline{\\mathbb R}$ be proper in the sense of [[def-tdmath-extended-convex-function]]. Its **Fenchel conjugate** is $f^*:\\mathbb R^d\\to\\overline{\\mathbb R}$,\n\n$$f^*(p):=\\sup_{x\\in\\operatorname{dom}f}\\bigl(\\langle p,x\\rangle-f(x)\\bigr).$$\n\nThe supremum is taken in $\\overline{\\mathbb R}$; its defining set is nonempty and consists of real numbers, so $f^*(p)\\in\\mathbb R\\cup\\{+\\infty\\}$. The **biconjugate** is an extended-real expression that may also take $-\\infty$ when its defining supremum is over the empty set:\n\n$$f^{**}(x):=\\sup_{\\{p\\in\\mathbb R^d:f^*(p)<+\\infty\\}}\\bigl(\\langle p,x\\rangle-f^*(p)\\bigr),$$\n\nwith the convention $\\sup\\varnothing=-\\infty$. For $x\\in\\operatorname{dom}f$, the **subdifferential** is\n\n$$\\partial f(x):=\\{p\\in\\mathbb R^d:f(y)\\ge f(x)+\\langle p,y-x\\rangle\\text{ for every }y\\in\\mathbb R^d\\};$$\n\nset $\\partial f(x)=\\varnothing$ for $x\\notin\\operatorname{dom}f$. A vector in $\\partial f(x)$ is a subgradient at $x$. All pairings use [[def-euclidean-inner-product]].",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.3",
            "2.1",
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L1",
          "source": "thm-of-archimedean",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Every complete ordered field $F$ ([[def-complete-ordered-field]]) is Archimedean:\nfor every $x \\in F$ there is a natural number $n \\ge 1$ with $x < n \\cdot 1_F$,\nwhere $n \\cdot 1_F$ is the canonical natural of the ordered field $F$\n([[def-ordered-field]]). Equivalently, the canonical naturals are cofinal in $F$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.2"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "For any $x$ with $f(x)<+\\infty$ and any $p$ with $f^*(p)<+\\infty$, the defining supremum gives $f^*(p)\\ge\\langle p,x\\rangle-f(x)$, hence $\\langle p,x\\rangle-f^*(p)\\le f(x)$; taking the supremum over $p$ yields $f^{**}(x)\\le f(x)$. If $f(x)=+\\infty$, the same upper bound is automatic.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F4",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "Fix $x\\in\\operatorname{dom}f$ and a real $t<f(x)$. Apply [F3] to $(x,t)$ and the nonempty closed convex set $\\operatorname{epi}f$, and negate the resulting separator to write $\\langle a,y\\rangle+bs\\ge c>\\langle a,x\\rangle+bt$ for all $(y,s)\\in\\operatorname{epi}f$. Upward closure forces $b\\ge0$. If $b=0$, the inequality at $(x,f(x))\\in\\operatorname{epi}f$ would give $\\langle a,x\\rangle\\ge c>\\langle a,x\\rangle$, impossible. Thus $b>0$.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-3",
          "claim": "Now fix $x\\notin\\operatorname{dom}f$ and $t\\in\\mathbb R$. Apply [F3] to $(x,t)$ and negate the separator to obtain $\\langle a,y\\rangle+bs\\ge c>\\langle a,x\\rangle+bt$ for all $(y,s)\\in\\operatorname{epi}f$. If $b>0$, then $f(y)\\ge-\\langle a/b,y\\rangle+c/b$ for every $y\\in\\operatorname{dom}f$, so with $p=-a/b$ one has $f^*(p)\\le-c/b<+\\infty$ and $f^{**}(x)\\ge\\langle p,x\\rangle-f^*(p)\\ge(c-\\langle a,x\\rangle)/b>t$.",
          "step": "1.3",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Substituting $(y,f(y))$ for every $y\\in\\operatorname{dom}f$ gives the affine minorant $\\ell(y)=-\\langle a/b,y\\rangle+c/b\\le f(y)$. With $p=-a/b$, [F4] gives $f^*(p)\\le-c/b$, hence $f^{**}(x)\\ge\\langle p,x\\rangle-f^*(p)\\ge\\ell(x)>t$. Since this holds for every $t<f(x)$, $f^{**}(x)\\ge f(x)$ on $\\operatorname{dom}f$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F4",
            "step 1.2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-2",
          "claim": "In the $b=0$ case of step 1.3, the separator gives $\\langle a,y\\rangle\\ge c>\\langle a,x\\rangle$ for every $y\\in\\operatorname{dom}f$. Choose $y_0\\in\\operatorname{dom}f$ and a finite $t_0<f(y_0)$. Apply [F3] to $(y_0,t_0)$ and $\\operatorname{epi}f$, reversing the separator orientation to obtain $\\langle a_0,y\\rangle+b_0s\\ge c_0>\\langle a_0,y_0\\rangle+b_0t_0$ for every $(y,s)\\in\\operatorname{epi}f$. Upward closure gives $b_0\\ge0$, and $b_0=0$ contradicts the inequality at $(y_0,f(y_0))$, so $b_0>0$. Set $p_0=-a_0/b_0$ and $r_0=c_0/b_0$; substituting $(y,f(y))$ gives the affine minorant $\\ell_0(y)=\\langle p_0,y\\rangle+r_0\\le f(y)$ on $\\operatorname{dom}f$, so [F4] gives $f^*(p_0)\\le-r_0$. For each positive integer $k$, define $\\ell_k(y)=\\ell_0(y)+k(c-\\langle a,y\\rangle)=\\langle p_0-ka,y\\rangle+(r_0+kc)$. Since $c-\\langle a,y\\rangle\\le0$ on $\\operatorname{dom}f$, this is still a minorant; [F4] gives $f^*(p_0-ka)\\le-(r_0+kc)<+\\infty$ and hence $f^{**}(x)\\ge\\ell_k(x)=\\ell_0(x)+k\\delta$, where $\\delta:=c-\\langle a,x\\rangle>0$ by step 1.3. For any prescribed $B\\in\\mathbb R$, apply [L1] to $q=(B-\\ell_0(x))/\\delta$ to choose a positive integer $k$ with $q<k$; multiplying by $\\delta>0$ gives $\\ell_0(x)+k\\delta>B$. Hence $f^{**}(x)$ exceeds every real bound, so $f^{**}(x)=+\\infty=f(x)$.",
          "step": "2.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "L1",
            "step 1.3",
            "choose",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "The upper bound from step 1.1 and the lower bounds from steps 2.1 and 2.2 cover respectively finite and infinite values of $f(x)$, proving equality everywhere.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "step 2.1",
            "step 2.2"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The Statement assumes f is proper, so its effective domain is nonempty."
