      ],
      "risk_review": {
        "status": "complete",
        "reviewer": "alpha-batch-4",
        "notes": "Reviewed six steps and all seven exact suppliers; Bianchi §2.2 Theorem 2.5 and its full proof, pp.15–16, agree with the stated finite-dimensional closed proper convex claim. Upper bound includes an empty finite-conjugate domain initially. At a finite-domain point the separator height coefficient is positive, yielding a finite affine minorant and nonempty conjugate domain. Outside the domain, upward closure again rules out negative height coefficients; positive coefficients handle any prescribed height. The zero coefficient case explicitly builds an independent affine minorant at y0, then adds k(c−<a,y>), remaining a minorant on the entire domain. Its value at x is unbounded by the exact Archimedean supplier. This covers vertical separators and infinite boundary values without assuming interior points or global lower bounds; no AC."
      }
    },
    "thm-tdmath-fenchel-attainment": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "L1",
          "source": "lem-tdmath-extended-convex-epigraph-equivalence",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $d\\ge1$ and let $f:\\mathbb R^d\\to\\overline{\\mathbb R}$ use the conventions of [[def-tdmath-extended-convex-function]]. Then $f$ is convex if and only if $\\operatorname{epi}f$ is a convex subset of $\\mathbb R^{d+1}$, and $f$ is lower semicontinuous if and only if $\\operatorname{epi}f$ is closed.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L2",
          "source": "def-euclidean-inner-product",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$. A natural number is a von Neumann natural, that is a set,\nand $n = \\{0, 1, \\dots, n-1\\}$ ([[def-natural-numbers]],\n[[lem-nat-order-is-membership]]), so\n\n$$\\mathbb{R}^{n} \\;=\\; \\{\\, x \\;:\\; x \\text{ is a function } n \\to \\mathbb{R} \\,\\}$$\n\nis the function space of [[def-function-space]] at $F = \\mathbb{R}$ and $X = n$,\na vector space over $\\mathbb{R}$ under the pointwise operations\n([[def-vector-space]]). We write $x_k := x(k)$ for $k < n$, and two elements of\n$\\mathbb{R}^{n}$ are equal exactly when they agree at every $k < n$. This is the\nsame set that [[lem-metrics-on-rn]] calls $\\mathbb{R}^{n}$.\n\nThe **Euclidean inner product** of $x, y \\in \\mathbb{R}^{n}$ is the real number\n\n$$\\langle x, y\\rangle \\;:=\\; \\sum_{k<n} x_k\\,y_k ,$$\n\nthe finite sum of [[def-finite-sum]] applied to the list $k \\mapsto x_k y_k$\n(extended by $0$ beyond $n$, as every finite list in this library is). The\n**Euclidean norm** of $x$ is\n\n$$\\lVert x\\rVert_2 \\;:=\\; \\sqrt{\\langle x, x\\rangle},$$\n\nwhich is defined because $\\langle x,x\\rangle = \\sum_{k<n} x_k^{2} \\ge 0$ (a sum\nof nonnegative terms, [[lem-finite-sum-laws]] clause 4 and\n[[lem-of-square-positive]], the case $x_k = 0$ giving $x_k^2 = 0$ by\n[[def-integer-power]]) and every nonnegative real has a unique nonnegative square\nroot ([[thm-of-square-roots]]).\n\n### Both are defined for every $n$, including $n = 0$\n\nAt $n = 0$ the set $\\mathbb{R}^{0}$ has exactly one element, the empty function,\nand it is the zero vector space ([[lem-standard-basis-of-f-n]] clause 5); the sum\nabove is the empty sum, so $\\langle x,y\\rangle = 0$ and $\\lVert x\\rVert_2 = 0$.\n**This is the first place on this page where the two index regimes diverge, and\nthe divergence is deliberate.** The published metrics $d_1$, $d_2$, $d_\\infty$ of\n[[lem-metrics-on-rn]] are defined only for $n \\ge 1$, because $d_\\infty$ would\notherwise be a maximum over the empty index set; the algebra above needs no such\nrestriction. The boundary in this page runs between the algebra and the metric,\nnot where a reader would guess, and [[rem-rn-conventions-and-scope]] lists\nexactly which items inherit $n \\ge 1$.\n\n### The algebra of the inner product\n\nFor all $x, y, z \\in \\mathbb{R}^{n}$ and $\\lambda \\in \\mathbb{R}$:\n\n1. **Symmetry.** $\\langle x,y\\rangle = \\langle y,x\\rangle$, since $x_ky_k = y_kx_k$\n   termwise.\n2. **Additivity in the first argument.**\n   $\\langle x + y, z\\rangle = \\langle x,z\\rangle + \\langle y,z\\rangle$: the list\n   $k \\mapsto (x_k+y_k)z_k$ is the termwise sum of $k \\mapsto x_kz_k$ and\n   $k \\mapsto y_kz_k$, so [[lem-finite-sum-laws]] clause 1 applies.\n3. **Homogeneity in the first argument.**\n   $\\langle \\lambda x, y\\rangle = \\lambda\\langle x,y\\rangle$, by\n   [[lem-finite-sum-laws]] clause 2.\n4. **Bilinearity.** Clauses 2 and 3 together with symmetry give the same two laws\n   in the second argument.\n5. **Positive definiteness.** $\\langle x,x\\rangle \\ge 0$, and\n   $\\langle x,x\\rangle = 0$ if and only if $x = 0$. Indeed a vanishing sum of\n   nonnegative terms has every term $0$ ([[lem-finite-sum-laws]] clause 4), so\n   $x_k^{2} = 0$ for every $k < n$, and a nonzero real has a positive square\n   ([[lem-of-square-positive]]), whence $x_k = 0$ for every $k < n$ and $x = 0$.\n6. **Agreement with the published Euclidean metric.** For $n \\ge 1$ and\n   $x, y \\in \\mathbb{R}^{n}$,\n   $\\lVert x - y\\rVert_2 = \\sqrt{\\sum_{k<n}(x_k-y_k)^{2}} = d_2(x,y)$, the two\n   sides being the same expression ([[lem-metrics-on-rn]]). In particular\n   $\\lVert x\\rVert_2 = d_2(x, 0)$.\n\nThat $\\lVert\\cdot\\rVert_2$ is a norm in the sense of [[def-norm-and-normed-space]]\nis proved in [[thm-cauchy-schwarz-and-the-euclidean-norm]], where the triangle\ninequality is obtained from the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality; it is not assumed\nhere.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L2",
          "source": "lem-euclidean-linear-maps-have-matrices-and-are-bounded",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For every linear $L:\\mathbb R^m\\to\\mathbb R^n$ there is a unique matrix $A=(a_{ij})_{i<n,j<m}$ such that $(Lh)_i=\\sum_{j<m}a_{ij}h_j$. Moreover there is $K\\ge0$ with $\\|Lh\\|_2\\le K\\|h\\|_2$ for every $h$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L3",
          "source": "thm-heine-borel-rn",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$ with $n \\ge 1$, let $\\mathbb{R}^n$ be the set of functions\n$n \\to \\mathbb{R}$ and let $d_2$ be the Euclidean metric on it\n([[lem-metrics-on-rn]]). Then:\n\n1. **Closed boxes are compact.** For reals $a_k \\le b_k$ $(k < n)$ the box\n   $Q = \\{\\, x \\in \\mathbb{R}^n : a_k \\le x_k \\le b_k \\text{ for every } k < n \\,\\}$\n   is a compact subset of $(\\mathbb{R}^n, d_2)$ ([[def-metric-compactness]]).\n2. **Heine-Borel.** A subset $K \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^n$ is a compact subset of\n   $(\\mathbb{R}^n, d_2)$ if and only if $K$ is closed in $\\mathbb{R}^n$\n   ([[def-metric-topology]]) and bounded\n   ([[def-metric-bounded-diameter]]).\n3. **The real line.** A subset $K \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$ is a compact subset of\n   $(\\mathbb{R}, d_{\\mathbb{R}})$, the usual metric $d_{\\mathbb{R}}(x,y) = |x-y|$\n   ([[lem-real-line-is-a-metric-space]]), if and only if $K$ is closed in\n   $\\mathbb{R}$ and bounded.\n\n**No choice principle is used.** The bisection below halves one coordinate at a\ntime and takes the **left** half whenever the left half still fails to be finitely\ncovered, the right half otherwise: a rule with two outcomes, decided by a\nproperty of the box, not a selection. That is the whole reason the theorem is\navailable in ZF, while the general \"complete and totally bounded implies compact\"\n([[thm-complete-and-totally-bounded-implies-compact]]) is not.\n\nThe hypothesis $n \\ge 1$ is inherited from [[lem-metrics-on-rn]], which defines\n$\\mathbb{R}^n$ and its metrics only there; the last remark below records what\nhappens at $n = 0$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L4",
          "source": "thm-compact-iff-finite-intersection-property",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X,d)$ be a metric space ([[def-metric-space]]), with closed sets as in\n[[def-metric-topology]] and the finite intersection property as in\n[[def-finite-intersection-property]]. For a family $\\mathcal{A}$ of subsets of\n$X$ write\n\n$$\\bigcap \\mathcal{A} \\;:=\\; \\{\\, x \\in X : x \\in F \\text{ for every } F \\in \\mathcal{A} \\,\\},$$\n\nso that $\\bigcap \\emptyset = X$, matching the convention for the empty finite\nintersection in [[def-finite-intersection-property]].\n\nThen $(X,d)$ is compact ([[def-metric-compactness]]) **if and only if** every\nfamily $\\mathcal{A}$ of closed subsets of $X$ with the finite intersection\nproperty satisfies $\\bigcap \\mathcal{A} \\ne \\emptyset$.\n\nNo choice principle is used in either direction: complementation is a canonical\nbijection, so no member of a family ever has to be selected.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L5",
          "source": "thm-infimum-property",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $S \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$ be nonempty and bounded below. Then $S$ has a\ngreatest lower bound in $\\mathbb{R}$ ([[def-infimum]]), and it is given by\n\n$$\\inf S = -\\sup(-S), \\qquad \\text{where } -S = \\{-s : s \\in S\\}.$$\n\nIn particular the complete ordered field $\\mathbb{R}$ has the\ngreatest-lower-bound property, which is therefore not an extra axiom: it is a\nconsequence of the least-upper-bound property.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L5",
          "source": "def-infimum",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $S \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$ and $\\ell \\in \\mathbb{R}$. Then $\\ell$ is a\n**greatest lower bound**, or **infimum**, of $S$ if both of the following hold:\n\n- $\\ell$ is a lower bound of $S$ ([[def-bounded-set]]), that is, $\\ell \\le s$ for\n  every $s \\in S$;\n- $\\ell' \\le \\ell$ for every lower bound $\\ell'$ of $S$.\n\nWritten out in one line:\n\n$$\\ell \\text{ is an infimum of } S \\iff \\big[(\\forall s \\in S)\\, \\ell \\le s\\big] \\text{ and } \\big[(\\forall \\ell' \\in \\mathbb{R})\\, \\big((\\forall s \\in S)\\, \\ell' \\le s\\big) \\Rightarrow \\ell' \\le \\ell\\big].$$\n\nAn infimum, when it exists, is unique ([[lem-sup-unique]]), so we may write\n$\\inf S$ for it.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L5",
          "source": "lem-inf-epsilon",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $S \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$ be nonempty and bounded below, and let $\\ell$ be a\nlower bound of $S$ ([[def-bounded-set]]). Then\n\n$$\\ell = \\inf S \\quad \\Longleftrightarrow \\quad \\text{for every } \\varepsilon > 0 \\text{ there exists } s \\in S \\text{ with } s < \\ell + \\varepsilon.$$\n\nIn words: among the lower bounds of $S$, the infimum is exactly the one that\ncannot be raised by any positive amount and still bound $S$ from below.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L6",
          "source": "lem-finite-set-has-max",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For every $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$ and all $a_0, a_1, \\dots, a_n \\in \\mathbb{R}$, the\nset $\\{a_0, a_1, \\dots, a_n\\}$ has a maximum and a minimum ([[def-max-min]]).\n\nWhat is proved below is exactly the displayed statement, by induction on $n$.\n\nThe usual reading, that *every nonempty finite subset of $\\mathbb{R}$ has a\nmaximum and a minimum*, follows once one identifies the nonempty finite subsets\nof $\\mathbb{R}$ with the sets listable as $\\{a_0, \\dots, a_n\\}$. That\nidentification is recorded as a stipulation in the Given below, because this page\nhas no definition of finiteness to prove it against. **It is discharged, not\nmerely assumed**: [[lem-finite-subsets-listable]] proves that the two\ndescriptions of a nonempty finite subset of $\\mathbb{R}$ agree. That lemma is\nrecorded in `justified_by` rather than in `deps`, since it is about the sets this\nlemma quantifies over and therefore depends on this one. This is what licenses\nthe notation\n$\\max\\{a_1, \\dots, a_n\\}$ and $\\min\\{a_1, \\dots, a_n\\}$ for finite sets of\n**real** numbers from this page onwards.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L7",
          "source": "lem-tdmath-fenchel-young-equality",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $f:\\mathbb R^d\\to\\overline{\\mathbb R}$ be proper, let $x\\in\\operatorname{dom}f$, and let $p\\in\\mathbb R^d$. Then $f^*(p)\\ge\\langle p,x\\rangle-f(x)$. Equality holds exactly when $p\\in\\partial f(x)$, equivalently when $x$ attains the supremum defining $f^*(p)$; in that case the supremum is finite.",
          "uses": [
            "6.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Since $f$ is proper, choose $x_0\\in\\operatorname{dom}f$ and put $c=g(x_0)\\in\\mathbb R$. By coercivity, first choose a radius $R_0>0$ such that $g(x)>c+1$ whenever $\\|x\\|_2\\ge R_0$, then enlarge it to $R>\\max\\{R_0,\\|x_0\\|_2\\}$; the same implication holds for this $R$. Let $K=\\{x:\\|x\\|_2\\le R\\}$; it is nonempty, closed, bounded, and therefore compact by [L3].",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "L3",
            "choose"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "The epigraph of $g$ is $T^{-1}(\\operatorname{epi}f)$, because $g(x)\\le r$ exactly when $f(x)\\le r+\\langle p,x\\rangle$. By [L1], $\\operatorname{epi}f$ is closed. Fix $z\\notin T^{-1}(\\operatorname{epi}f)$; openness of the complement of $\\operatorname{epi}f$ gives $\\varepsilon>0$ such that the Euclidean ball of radius $\\varepsilon$ around $Tz$ misses $\\operatorname{epi}f$. Set $\\rho=\\varepsilon/(K+1)>0$. If $\\|z'-z\\|_2<\\rho$, [L2] gives $\\|Tz'-Tz\\|_2\\le K\\|z'-z\\|_2<\\varepsilon$, so $Tz'$ also lies outside $\\operatorname{epi}f$. Thus the complement of $T^{-1}(\\operatorname{epi}f)$ is open and $\\operatorname{epi}g$ is closed. By [L1], $g$ is lower semicontinuous.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "L1",
            "L2",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "For each real $r>0$, $U_r=\\{x\\in K:g(x)>-r\\}$ is relatively open in $K$, since its complement is the weak sublevel $K\\cap\\{g\\le-r\\}$. These sets cover $K$ because $g(x)\\in\\mathbb R\\cup\\{+\\infty\\}$ for every $x$ (properness excludes $-\\infty$ and the tilt is finite). Compactness gives a finite subcover, and [L6] supplies a largest real index $r_*$ among its finitely many indices. Since the $U_r$ are nested, $U_{r_*}=K$, so every finite value of $g$ on $K$ is greater than $-r_*$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "L1",
            "L3",
            "L6",
            "given",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "Let $S:=\\{g(x):x\\in K,\\ g(x)<+\\infty\\}$. It is nonempty because $x_0\\in K$ and $g(x_0)=c$, and it is a real set bounded below by $-r_*$ by step 2.1. It therefore has a real infimum $m$. For every $\\varepsilon>0$, [L5] gives $s\\in S$ with $s<m+\\varepsilon$; choosing $x\\in K$ with $g(x)=s$ shows $F_\\varepsilon:=K\\cap\\{x:g(x)\\le m+\\varepsilon\\}$ is nonempty. It is closed in $K$ by [L1]. Any nonempty finite subfamily has nonempty intersection, because the member with least positive $\\varepsilon$ is contained in all the others; the empty subfamily has intersection $K\\ne\\emptyset$.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "L1",
            "L4",
            "L5",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 2.1",
            "choose"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "claim": "Applying [L4] to the family $(F_\\varepsilon)_{\\varepsilon>0}$ gives $x_*\\in\\bigcap_{\\varepsilon>0}F_\\varepsilon$. The bound $g(x_*)\\le m+1$ makes $g(x_*)$ finite, so $g(x_*)\\in S$ and $m\\le g(x_*)$. If $g(x_*)>m$, choose $\\varepsilon=(g(x_*)-m)/2>0$; then $x_*\\in F_\\varepsilon$ would give $g(x_*)\\le m+\\varepsilon<g(x_*)$, a contradiction. Thus $g(x_*)=m$.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "L4",
            "step 3.1",
            "algebra",
            "choose"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-5-1",
          "claim": "For $x\\notin K$, step 1.1 gives $g(x)>c+1\\ge m+1$, because $x_0\\in K$ implies $m\\le c$. Thus $x_*$ is a global minimizer over $\\mathbb R^d$, and its value $m$ is finite.",
          "step": "5.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "step 4.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-6-1",
          "claim": "On $\\operatorname{dom}f$, $-g(x)=\\langle p,x\\rangle-f(x)$, and outside this domain $g(x)=+\\infty$. The global minimum $m$ is attained at $x_*\\in\\operatorname{dom}f$, so the supremum in the definition of $f^*(p)$ is finite, equals $-m$, and is attained at $x_*$. Therefore $f^*(p)=\\langle p,x_*\\rangle-f(x_*)$; [L7] yields $p\\in\\partial f(x_*)$.",
          "step": "6.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "L7",
            "step 5.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "Properness excludes an empty effective domain."
        },
