        }
      ],
      "risk_review": {
        "status": "complete",
        "reviewer": "alpha-batch-4",
        "notes": "Reviewed seven steps and all fourteen exact suppliers. Properness supplies a finite x0; coercivity isolates the search in a nonempty compact ball. The bounded shear maps epi(g) to epi(f), and its explicit ball estimate proves lsc. Relatively open sets {g>−r} cover the ball, yielding a finite lower bound with a nonempty finite maximum; the family of closed near-infimum sublevels has FIP including the empty subfamily. Compact FIP gives a finite attained infimum; outside-ball coercivity gives the global minimum, and Fenchel equality yields the finite conjugate maximum and subgradient. No countable selection or uniqueness is inferred. d≥1 is the exact Heine–Borel scope. Bianchi §1.5 Proposition 1.23 and §2.3 Proposition 2.9 are consistent source context."
      }
    },
    "thm-tdmath-finite-mixture-envelope": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "thm-heine-borel-rn",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$ with $n \\ge 1$, let $\\mathbb{R}^n$ be the set of functions\n$n \\to \\mathbb{R}$ and let $d_2$ be the Euclidean metric on it\n([[lem-metrics-on-rn]]). Then:\n\n1. **Closed boxes are compact.** For reals $a_k \\le b_k$ $(k < n)$ the box\n   $Q = \\{\\, x \\in \\mathbb{R}^n : a_k \\le x_k \\le b_k \\text{ for every } k < n \\,\\}$\n   is a compact subset of $(\\mathbb{R}^n, d_2)$ ([[def-metric-compactness]]).\n2. **Heine-Borel.** A subset $K \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^n$ is a compact subset of\n   $(\\mathbb{R}^n, d_2)$ if and only if $K$ is closed in $\\mathbb{R}^n$\n   ([[def-metric-topology]]) and bounded\n   ([[def-metric-bounded-diameter]]).\n3. **The real line.** A subset $K \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$ is a compact subset of\n   $(\\mathbb{R}, d_{\\mathbb{R}})$, the usual metric $d_{\\mathbb{R}}(x,y) = |x-y|$\n   ([[lem-real-line-is-a-metric-space]]), if and only if $K$ is closed in\n   $\\mathbb{R}$ and bounded.\n\n**No choice principle is used.** The bisection below halves one coordinate at a\ntime and takes the **left** half whenever the left half still fails to be finitely\ncovered, the right half otherwise: a rule with two outcomes, decided by a\nproperty of the box, not a selection. That is the whole reason the theorem is\navailable in ZF, while the general \"complete and totally bounded implies compact\"\n([[thm-complete-and-totally-bounded-implies-compact]]) is not.\n\nThe hypothesis $n \\ge 1$ is inherited from [[lem-metrics-on-rn]], which defines\n$\\mathbb{R}^n$ and its metrics only there; the last remark below records what\nhappens at $n = 0$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-continuous-image-of-a-compact-space-is-compact",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X,d_X)$ and $(Y,d_Y)$ be metric spaces ([[def-metric-space]]) and let\n$f : X \\to Y$ be continuous ([[def-metric-continuity]]). Then:\n\n1. If $(X,d_X)$ is compact ([[def-metric-compactness]]), the image\n   $f[X] \\subseteq Y$ is a compact subset of $Y$.\n2. More generally, if $K \\subseteq X$ is a compact subset of $X$, then $f[K]$ is\n   a compact subset of $Y$.\n\nNo choice principle is used.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-extreme-value-metric",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X,d)$ be a nonempty compact metric space ([[def-metric-compactness]],\n[[def-metric-space]]) and let $f : X \\to \\mathbb{R}$ be continuous\n([[def-metric-continuity]]), $\\mathbb{R}$ carrying its usual metric\n$d_{\\mathbb{R}}(s,t) = |s-t|$ ([[lem-real-line-is-a-metric-space]]). Then the\nimage $f[X]$ is bounded above and below ([[def-bounded-set]]), and it has a\nmaximum and a minimum ([[def-max-min]]): there are points\n$x_{\\max}, x_{\\min} \\in X$ with\n\n$$f(x_{\\min}) \\;\\le\\; f(x) \\;\\le\\; f(x_{\\max}) \\qquad \\text{for every } x \\in X,$$\n\nand then $f(x_{\\max}) = \\sup f[X]$ and $f(x_{\\min}) = \\inf f[X]$\n([[def-complete-ordered-field]], [[def-infimum]]).\n\nNonemptiness of $X$ is a hypothesis and not an oversight: for $X = \\emptyset$ the\nimage is empty and has neither a supremum nor a maximum. No choice principle is\nused.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "lem-tdmath-extended-convex-epigraph-equivalence",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $d\\ge1$ and let $f:\\mathbb R^d\\to\\overline{\\mathbb R}$ use the conventions of [[def-tdmath-extended-convex-function]]. Then $f$ is convex if and only if $\\operatorname{epi}f$ is a convex subset of $\\mathbb R^{d+1}$, and $f$ is lower semicontinuous if and only if $\\operatorname{epi}f$ is closed.",
          "uses": [
            "2.2",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "def-convex-subset-of-euclidean-space",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "A subset $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^m$ is **convex** when, for all $x,y\\in U$ and $t\\in[0,1]$ ([[def-interval]]), the point $(1-t)x+ty$ lies in $U$. Thus the full line segment from $x$ to $y$ remains in $U$.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "thm-tdmath-finite-dimensional-biconjugation",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $d\\ge1$ and let $f:\\mathbb R^d\\to\\overline{\\mathbb R}$ be closed, proper, and convex in the sense of [[def-tdmath-extended-convex-function]]. With the Fenchel conjugate and biconjugate of [[def-tdmath-fenchel-transform-and-subgradient]], one has\n\n$$f^{**}(x)=f(x)\\qquad(x\\in\\mathbb R^d).$$",
          "uses": [
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "lem-tdmath-fenchel-young-equality",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $f:\\mathbb R^d\\to\\overline{\\mathbb R}$ be proper, let $x\\in\\operatorname{dom}f$, and let $p\\in\\mathbb R^d$. Then $f^*(p)\\ge\\langle p,x\\rangle-f(x)$. Equality holds exactly when $p\\in\\partial f(x)$, equivalently when $x$ attains the supremum defining $f^*(p)$; in that case the supremum is finite.",
          "uses": [
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L1",
          "source": "lem-euclidean-linear-maps-have-matrices-and-are-bounded",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For every linear $L:\\mathbb R^m\\to\\mathbb R^n$ there is a unique matrix $A=(a_{ij})_{i<n,j<m}$ such that $(Lh)_i=\\sum_{j<m}a_{ij}h_j$. Moreover there is $K\\ge0$ with $\\|Lh\\|_2\\le K\\|h\\|_2$ for every $h$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "The set $\\Delta_m$ contains each coordinate vector. The coordinate maps, the sum map $\\theta\\mapsto\\sum_i\\theta_i$, and $Q$ are continuous by [L1]. For any continuous map $T$ into a Euclidean space and any closed set $K$, the complement of $T^{-1}(K)$ is open: if $T(z)\\notin K$, a ball about $T(z)$ misses $K$, and continuity gives a ball about $z$ mapped inside that ball. Thus $T^{-1}(K)$ is closed. Coordinate half-lines $[0,\\infty)$ and singletons are closed, since a point outside either has a positive-distance ball missing it; a finite intersection of closed sets is closed because its complement is a finite union of open sets. These facts show that $\\Delta_m$ is closed and that $\\Theta_x=\\Delta_m\\cap Q^{-1}(\\{x\\})$ is closed. Since $0\\le\\theta_i\\le1$ on $\\Delta_m$, both sets are bounded and hence compact by [F1]. For $x\\in P$, the definition of $P$ makes $\\Theta_x$ nonempty. The linear objective is continuous by [L1] and attains its minimum on $\\Theta_x$ by [F3].",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F3",
            "L1",
            "given",
            "construct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Let $C=\\{\\sum_i\\theta_i(q_i,a_i):\\theta\\in\\Delta_m\\}\\subset\\mathbb R^{d+1}$ and $V=\\{(0,s):s\\ge0\\}$. The affine map defining $C$ is continuous by [L1], so $C$ is compact by [F2]. The set $C$ is convex because convex combinations of points in $\\Delta_m$ remain in $\\Delta_m$ (nonnegativity and the unit-sum equation are preserved) and the defining map is affine; $V$ is closed and convex. If $(x,r)\\in\\operatorname{epi}f_{\\rm mix}$, a minimizing $\\theta\\in\\Theta_x$ gives $(x,f_{\\rm mix}(x))\\in C$ and $r-f_{\\rm mix}(x)\\ge0$, so $(x,r)\\in C+V$. Conversely if $(x,r)=\\sum_i\\theta_i(q_i,a_i)+(0,s)$ with $s\\ge0$, then $f_{\\rm mix}(x)\\le\\sum_i\\theta_i a_i\\le r$. Thus $\\operatorname{epi}f_{\\rm mix}=C+V$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "L1",
            "step 1.1",
            "construct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-2",
          "claim": "The coordinate vector with $\\theta_i=1$ and all other weights zero shows $f_{\\rm mix}(q_i)\\le a_i$. Let $h:\\mathbb R^d\\to\\overline{\\mathbb R}$ be convex with $h(q_i)\\le a_i$. For any weights $\\theta\\in\\Delta_m$, prove $h(\\sum_i\\theta_iq_i)\\le\\sum_i\\theta_i h(q_i)$ by induction on the number $N$ of positive weights: for $N=1$ it is equality; for $N>1$, choose an index $j$ with $0<\\theta_j<1$, put $y=(1-\\theta_j)^{-1}\\sum_{i\\ne j}\\theta_iq_i$, and apply the induction hypothesis to the normalized remaining weights. Convexity then gives $h(\\sum_i\\theta_iq_i)\\le\\theta_jh(q_j)+(1-\\theta_j)h(y)\\le\\sum_i\\theta_i h(q_i)$. All terms on the right are finite because $h(q_i)\\le a_i$ and the codomain excludes $-\\infty$. For $x\\in P$ and any $\\theta\\in\\Theta_x$, this gives $h(x)\\le\\sum_i\\theta_i a_i$; taking the minimum yields $h(x)\\le f_{\\rm mix}(x)$. For $x\\notin P$, $h(x)\\le+\\infty=f_{\\rm mix}(x)$. Thus $f_{\\rm mix}$ is the greatest convex minorant of the data.",
          "step": "2.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F4",
            "given",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra",
            "induction"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "The set $C+V$ is convex: for $c_1,c_2\\in C$, $v_1,v_2\\in V$, and $t\\in[0,1]$, $(1-t)(c_1+v_1)+t(c_2+v_2)=((1-t)c_1+tc_2)+((1-t)v_1+tv_2)\\in C+V$ by [F5]. To see it is closed, fix $z=(x,r)\\notin C+V$ and write $c=(u,a)\\in C$. The distance from $z$ to the ray $c+V$ is $\\rho_z(c)=\\sqrt{\\|x-u\\|_2^2+\\max\\{a-r,0\\}^2}$: if $r\\ge a$ the closest ray point has height $r$, and if $r<a$ it has height $a$. This formula is continuous in $c$ because $\\max\\{w,0\\}=(w+|w|)/2$, and it is positive for every $c\\in C$ since $z\\notin c+V$. Compactness of $C$ and [F3] give a minimum $\\delta>0$; every point of $C+V$ is at distance at least $\\delta$ from $z$, so the ball of radius $\\delta/2$ about $z$ misses $C+V$.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F5",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "claim": "By [F4] and step 3.1, $f_{\\rm mix}$ is convex and lower semicontinuous. It is proper because $P$ is nonempty, each value on $P$ is finite by step 1.1, and it is $+\\infty$ outside $P$. Therefore [F6] gives $f_{\\rm mix}=f_{\\rm mix}^{**}$.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F4",
            "F6",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-5-1",
          "claim": "Let $p\\in\\partial f_{\\rm mix}(x)$ and let $\\theta$ attain the minimum defining $f_{\\rm mix}(x)$. By [F7], $f_{\\rm mix}^*(p)=\\langle p,x\\rangle-f_{\\rm mix}(x)$. Also $f_{\\rm mix}(q_i)\\le a_i$ implies $\\langle p,q_i\\rangle-a_i\\le\\langle p,q_i\\rangle-f_{\\rm mix}(q_i)\\le f_{\\rm mix}^*(p)$. Since $\\langle p,x\\rangle-f_{\\rm mix}(x)=\\sum_i\\theta_i(\\langle p,q_i\\rangle-a_i)$ is a weighted average of terms bounded above by $f_{\\rm mix}^*(p)$, each term with $\\theta_i>0$ equals that bound, proving the supporting-hyperplane formula.",
          "step": "5.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F7",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 2.2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
