    "lem-tdmath-stoichiometric-tangent-cone": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-total-derivative-in-euclidean-space",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^m$ be open, let $a\\in U$, and let $f:U\\to\\mathbb R^n$. The map $f$ is **totally differentiable at $a$** when there is a linear map $L:\\mathbb R^m\\to\\mathbb R^n$ ([[def-euclidean-linear-map]]) such that\n\n$$\\lim_{h\\to0}\\frac{\\|f(a+h)-f(a)-Lh\\|_2}{\\|h\\|_2}=0,$$\n\nwhere the quotient is considered for $h\\ne0$ with $a+h\\in U$. The map $L$, when it exists, is denoted $Df(a)$ and called the **total derivative**. Equivalently, $f(a+h)=f(a)+Df(a)h+r(h)$ with $\\|r(h)\\|_2/\\|h\\|_2\\to0$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-convex-subset-of-euclidean-space",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "A subset $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^m$ is **convex** when, for all $x,y\\in U$ and $t\\in[0,1]$ ([[def-interval]]), the point $(1-t)x+ty$ lies in $U$. Thus the full line segment from $x$ to $y$ remains in $U$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $f:U\\to V\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ be totally differentiable at $a\\in U$ and let $g:V\\to\\mathbb R^p$ be totally differentiable at $f(a)$. Then $g\\circ f$ is totally differentiable at $a$ and\n\n$$D(g\\circ f)(a)=Dg(f(a))\\circ Df(a).$$",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "def-convex-and-strictly-convex-functions-on-euclidean-sets",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $C\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ be convex ([[def-convex-subset-of-euclidean-space]]). The function $f:C\\to\\mathbb R$ is convex when $f((1-t)x+ty)\\le(1-t)f(x)+tf(y)$ for all $x,y\\in C$ and $t\\in[0,1]$. The parameter interval is the closed interval of [[def-interval]].\n\nIt is strictly convex when the inequality is strict for distinct $x,y$ and $0<t<1$. No strict inequality is required at $t=0$ or $t=1$, where the two sides coincide. The empty set and a singleton support convex functions, and strict convexity on either is vacuous.",
          "uses": [
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "lem-euclidean-linear-maps-have-matrices-and-are-bounded",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For every linear $L:\\mathbb R^m\\to\\mathbb R^n$ there is a unique matrix $A=(a_{ij})_{i<n,j<m}$ such that $(Lh)_i=\\sum_{j<m}a_{ij}h_j$. Moreover there is $K\\ge0$ with $\\|Lh\\|_2\\le K\\|h\\|_2$ for every $h$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Suppose $h\\in T_{\\mathcal F}(\\xi_*)$, and choose sequences $t_k,h_k$ from its definition. For each active $j\\in J$, feasibility gives $0+t_k(\\nu h_k)_j\\ge0$, hence $(\\nu h_k)_j\\ge0$. Since [F5] makes the finite matrix map continuous, $h_k\\to h$ implies $\\nu h_k\\to\\nu h$, so $(\\nu h)_j\\ge0$. This proves one inclusion.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F5",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "Conversely suppose $(\\nu h)_j\\ge0$ for all $j\\in J$. For each inactive $j$, put $b_j=n^0_j+(\\nu\\xi_*)_j>0$. If $(\\nu h)_j<0$, restrict $0\\le t\\le b_j/[-(\\nu h)_j]$; if $(\\nu h)_j\\ge0$, no restriction is needed. There are finitely many species, so choose a common $\\epsilon>0$ no larger than all positive bounds (or $\\epsilon=1$ if there are none). For $j\\in J$, $n^0_j+(\\nu(\\xi_*+th))_j=t(\\nu h)_j\\ge0$; for inactive $j$, the choice of $\\epsilon$ gives $b_j+t(\\nu h)_j\\ge0$ for $0\\le t\\le\\epsilon$. Thus $\\xi_*+th\\in\\mathcal F$ throughout that interval. Taking $h_k=h$ and any positive $t_k\\le\\epsilon$ tending to zero proves $h\\in T_{\\mathcal F}(\\xi_*)$.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "choose",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "By steps 1.1–1.2, every tangent $h$ has a feasible ray $\\xi_*+th$ for all sufficiently small $t\\ge0$. Openness of $U$ keeps the initial part of this ray in $U$. At a relative local minimum, $[f(\\xi_*+th)-f(\\xi_*)]/t\\ge0$ for all sufficiently small $t>0$; differentiability [F1] makes its limit $Df(\\xi_*)h$. Hence $Df(\\xi_*)h\\ge0$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 1.2",
            "given"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-2",
          "claim": "Now assume $U$ is convex and $f$ convex and differentiable. For any $\\xi\\in\\mathcal F\\cap U$, put $h=\\xi-\\xi_*$. For active $j$, $(\\nu h)_j=n_j(\\xi)-n_j(\\xi_*)=n_j(\\xi)\\ge0$, so step 1.2 makes $h$ a tangent direction; the segment $\\xi_*+th$ also lies in $\\mathcal F\\cap U$ by [F2]. Let $g(t)=f(\\xi_*+th)$. [F4] gives $(g(t)-g(0))/t\\le g(1)-g(0)$ for $0<t<1$; as $t\\downarrow0$, the left side tends to $Df(\\xi_*)h$ by [F1]. If every tangent derivative is nonnegative, then $f(\\xi)-f(\\xi_*)=g(1)-g(0)\\ge Df(\\xi_*)h\\ge0$.",
          "step": "2.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F4",
            "step 1.2",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "The affine map $\\xi\\mapsto n^0+\\nu\\xi$ has derivative $h\\mapsto\\nu h$. By [F3], $Df(\\xi_*)h=D\\Phi(n_*)(\\nu h)$. Since $\\nu h=\\sum_{j=1}^s(\\nu h)_j e_j$ and $D\\Phi(n_*)$ is linear, this equals $\\sum_{j=1}^s(\\nu h)_jD\\Phi(n_*)e_j=\\sum_{j=1}^s\\mu_j(\\nu h)_j=(\\nu^{\\mathsf T}\\mu)\\cdot h$, as claimed.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F3",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Step 1.2 covers an empty active set; its finite-coordinate construction takes ε=1 when no inactive coordinate restricts the ray."
        },
        {
          "case": "zero",
