    "thm-tdmath-local-implicit-rank": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-c-one-map-and-local-inverse",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^m$ be open and $f:U\\to\\mathbb R^n$. The map $f$ is\n**continuously differentiable**, or of class $C^1$, when it is totally\ndifferentiable at every point of $U$ and the entries of its derivative matrix\nare continuous functions on $U$.\n\nFor an open $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ and $a\\in U$, a **local inverse of $f$ at\n$a$** is a function $g:W\\to V$ for open neighbourhoods $a\\in V\\subseteq U$\nand $f(a)\\in W\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ such that $f|_V:V\\to W$ is bijective and\n$g=(f|_V)^{-1}$. If both $f|_V$ and $g$ are $C^1$, this restriction is a\n**local diffeomorphism** at $a$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-invertible-euclidean-linear-map",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $n\\ge1$.  A linear map $A:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R^n$\n([[def-euclidean-linear-map]]) is **invertible** when there is a linear map\n$B:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R^n$ such that\n\n$$B(Au)=u\\quad\\hbox{and}\\quad A(Bu)=u\\qquad(u\\in\\mathbb R^n).$$\n\nThe map $B$ is unique: if $C$ has the same two properties, then\n$C=C\\circ(A\\circ B)=(C\\circ A)\\circ B=B$.  It is denoted $A^{-1}$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L1",
          "source": "thm-euclidean-implicit-function-theorem",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $m,n\\ge1$, let $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^{m+n}$ be open, and let\n$F:U\\to\\mathbb R^n$ be $C^1$.  Suppose $(a,b)\\in U$, $F(a,b)=0$, and the\n**partial derivative in the second block**\n\n$$D_yF(a,b):\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R^n,\\qquad D_yF(a,b)v:=DF(a,b)(0,v),$$\n\nis invertible.  Put similarly $D_xF(a,b)u:=DF(a,b)(u,0)$.  Then there are open\nneighbourhoods $P$ of $a$ and $Q$ of $b$, and a unique $C^1$ map\n$\\varphi:P\\to Q$, such that\n\n$$F(x,y)=0\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad y=\\varphi(x)\\qquad((x,y)\\in P\\times Q).$$\n\nAfter shrinking $P,Q$ if necessary, $D_yF(x,\\varphi(x))$ is invertible and\n\n$$D\\varphi(x)=-D_yF(x,\\varphi(x))^{-1}D_xF(x,\\varphi(x)).$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L2",
          "source": "thm-euclidean-inverse-function-theorem",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $n\\ge1$, let $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ be open, let $f:U\\to\\mathbb R^n$ be\n$C^1$, and let $a\\in U$.  If $Df(a)$ is invertible, then there are open sets\n$V,W\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ with $a\\in V\\subseteq U$ and $f(a)\\in W$ such that\n$f|_V:V\\to W$ is bijective.  Its inverse $g:W\\to V$ is $C^1$, and\n\n$$Dg(y)=Df(g(y))^{-1}\\qquad(y\\in W).$$\n\nThus $f$ is a local diffeomorphism at $a$.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L3",
          "source": "lem-euclidean-linear-maps-have-matrices-and-are-bounded",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For every linear $L:\\mathbb R^m\\to\\mathbb R^n$ there is a unique matrix $A=(a_{ij})_{i<n,j<m}$ such that $(Lh)_i=\\sum_{j<m}a_{ij}h_j$. Moreover there is $K\\ge0$ with $\\|Lh\\|_2\\le K\\|h\\|_2$ for every $h$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L4",
          "source": "thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $f:U\\to V\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ be totally differentiable at $a\\in U$ and let $g:V\\to\\mathbb R^p$ be totally differentiable at $f(a)$. Then $g\\circ f$ is totally differentiable at $a$ and\n\n$$D(g\\circ f)(a)=Dg(f(a))\\circ Df(a).$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L4",
          "source": "def-metric-topology",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(X,d)$ be a metric space ([[def-metric-space]]).\n\nA subset $U \\subseteq X$ is **open in $(X,d)$** if for every $x \\in U$ there is a\nreal $r > 0$ with $B(x,r) \\subseteq U$ ([[def-metric-ball]]). A subset\n$F \\subseteq X$ is **closed in $(X,d)$** if its complement $X \\setminus F$ is\nopen.\n\nThe collection\n\n$$\\mathcal{T}_d := \\{\\, U \\subseteq X : U \\text{ is open in } (X,d) \\,\\}$$\n\nof all open subsets is the **metric topology** of $d$ on $X$. A subset of $X$\nthat is both open and closed is called **clopen**.\n\n**Two sets are open for trivial reasons.** $\\emptyset$ is open, because the\ndefining condition quantifies over no points; and $X$ is open, because\n$B(x,r) \\subseteq X$ for every $x$ and every $r > 0$. Consequently $X$ and\n$\\emptyset$ are also closed, and both are clopen.\n\n**A neighbourhood of a point $x$** is any set containing an open set that\ncontains $x$; an **open neighbourhood** is an open set containing $x$. In a\nmetric space, a set is a neighbourhood of $x$ exactly when it contains some\nball $B(x,r)$ with $r>0$. Thus $U$ is open exactly when it is a neighbourhood of\neach of its points, and metric balls suffice to test this condition.\n\n**The metric, not the set, determines $\\mathcal{T}_d$.** Two metrics on the same\nset may have different metric topologies, and two different metrics may have the\nsame one; the systematic comparison is [[def-equivalent-metrics]].",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "First suppose $q<n$. Choose the coordinate permutation that puts the columns of the stated invertible minor into the second block $v\\in\\mathbb R^q$, and write the remaining coordinates as $u\\in\\mathbb R^{n-q}$. Composing $F$ with the inverse permutation preserves openness and the $C^1$ property by [F1], [L3], and [L4]; its second-block derivative at $(u_*,v_*)$ is exactly the chosen invertible minor in the sense of [F2]. Since $n-q\\ge1$ and $q\\ge1$, [L1] applies and gives the neighborhoods, unique graph $\\varphi$, equivalence, and displayed derivative formula.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "L1",
            "L3",
            "L4",
            "given",
            "construct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "The graph map $u\\mapsto(u,\\varphi(u))$ is one-to-one because its first coordinate is $u$, and its inverse on the graph is coordinate projection. Its parameter domain $P$ is open in $\\mathbb R^{n-q}$, so this is the claimed local graph dimension.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "Now suppose $q=n$. The selected minor is the whole square derivative matrix $DF(x_*)$, so it is invertible by [F2]. Apply [L2] on neighborhoods $V$ of $x_*$ and $W$ of $0$. Injectivity of $F|_V$ and $F(x_*)=0$ imply $F(x)=0$ for $x\\in V$ exactly when $x=x_*$. Thus the local zero set is a singleton and has the stated zero-dimensional graph form.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "L2",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
