    "thm-tdmath-phase-dimension": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-c-one-map-and-local-inverse",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^m$ be open and $f:U\\to\\mathbb R^n$. The map $f$ is\n**continuously differentiable**, or of class $C^1$, when it is totally\ndifferentiable at every point of $U$ and the entries of its derivative matrix\nare continuous functions on $U$.\n\nFor an open $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ and $a\\in U$, a **local inverse of $f$ at\n$a$** is a function $g:W\\to V$ for open neighbourhoods $a\\in V\\subseteq U$\nand $f(a)\\in W\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ such that $f|_V:V\\to W$ is bijective and\n$g=(f|_V)^{-1}$. If both $f|_V$ and $g$ are $C^1$, this restriction is a\n**local diffeomorphism** at $a$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L1",
          "source": "thm-tdmath-local-implicit-rank",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $1\\le q\\le n$, let $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ be open, and let $F:U\\to\\mathbb R^q$ be $C^1$ in the sense of [[def-c-one-map-and-local-inverse]]. Suppose $F(x_*)=0$ and some $q\\times q$ minor of the derivative matrix $DF(x_*)$ is invertible. This nonsingular-minor hypothesis is the explicit full-row-rank condition used here. If $q<n$, then after permuting coordinates $x=(u,v)\\in\\mathbb R^{n-q}\\times\\mathbb R^q$, there are open neighborhoods $P$ of $u_*$ and $Q$ of $v_*$ and a unique $C^1$ map $\\varphi:P\\to Q$ such that\n\n$$F(u,v)=0\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad v=\\varphi(u)\\qquad((u,v)\\in P\\times Q).$$\n\nIts derivative is\n\n$$D\\varphi(u)=-D_vF(u,\\varphi(u))^{-1}D_uF(u,\\varphi(u)).$$\n\nThus the local zero set is a graph over $n-q$ free coordinates. If $q=n$, the zero is locally isolated, so the local graph dimension is zero. Here “local graph dimension $k$” means a local graph parameterized by an open subset of $\\mathbb R^k$, with a singleton counted as dimension zero.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L2",
          "source": "thm-algebra-of-total-derivatives",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "If $f,g:U\\to\\mathbb R^n$ are totally differentiable at $a$ and $c\\in\\mathbb R$, then $f+g$ and $cf$ are totally differentiable at $a$, with\n\n$$D(f+g)(a)=Df(a)+Dg(a),\\qquad D(cf)(a)=cDf(a).$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "For $P\\ge2$, $F$ has one scalar component for each pair $(i,\\alpha)$ with $1\\le i\\le C$ and $2\\le\\alpha\\le P$, so it has $q=C(P-1)$ components. Each is a difference of $C^1$ maps and therefore $F$ is $C^1$ on the open set $U$ by [F1, L2]. The assumed minor lets [L1] apply, giving local graph dimension $n-q$. Expanding $n=2+P(C-1)$ gives $n-q=2+PC-P-CP+C=C-P+2$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "L1",
            "L2",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "If $P=1$, no phase-equality equations are indexed because there is no $\\alpha\\ge2$. The local solution set is all of the open domain $U$, which has $n=2+(C-1)=C+1=C-P+2$ coordinates.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "In the reactive variant, each added affinity is a finite linear combination of $C^1$ coordinate functions and is therefore $C^1$ by [F1, L2]; the combined map has $q+R$ scalar equations. Its stated minor satisfies [L1], so the graph dimension is $n-q-R=2+P(C-1)-C(P-1)-R=C-P+2-R$. Linear independence of the $\\nu^r$ identifies $R$ distinct reaction directions, while the full-rank minor remains a separate necessary assumption on the equation derivatives.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "L1",
            "L2",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement C,P>=1 exclude an empty component or phase list. C=1 gives R^0={0} and Omega_1={0}; P=1 gives no phase-equality rows. These explicit conventions retain a nonempty coordinate domain.",
          "case_scope": {
            "source_section": "Statement",
            "source_excerpt": "Let $C\\ge1$ be the number of components and $P\\ge1$ the number of phases. Use common positive real coordinates $T,p$, expressed in fixed units if they are given a physical interpretation, and, for each phase $\\alpha$, an interior composition vector\n\n$$x^\\alpha\\in\\Omega_C:=\\{x\\in\\mathbb R^{C-1}:x_i>0,\\ \\sum_{i=1}^{C-1}x_i<1\\},$$\n\nwith the convention $\\mathbb R^0=\\{0\\}$ and $\\Omega_1=\\{0\\}$. The final component fraction is $1-\\sum_{i<C}x_i>0$. The ambient coordinate domain $U=(0,\\infty)^2\\times\\Omega_C^P$ is open in $\\mathbb R^n$, where $n=2+P(C-1)$. For each phase $\\alpha$ and component $i$, let $\\mu_i^\\alpha(T,p,x^\\alpha)$ be a real-valued $C^1$ coordinate function; these are mathematical maps and no thermodynamic potential or physical realization is assumed.\n\nFor $P\\ge2$, define the $q=C(P-1)$ coexistence equations\n\n$$F_{i,\\alpha}(T,p,x^1,\\ldots,x^P)=\\mu_i^\\alpha(T,p,x^\\alpha)-\\mu_i^1(T,p,x^1)=0\\quad(1\\le i\\le C,\\ 2\\le\\alpha\\le P).$$\n\nAt a solution $z_*\\in U$, assume some $q\\times q$ derivative minor of $F:U\\to\\mathbb R^q$ is invertible. Then the local solution set is a graph of dimension\n\n$$n-q=2+P(C-1)-C(P-1)=C-P+2.$$\n\nFor $P=1$ there are no coexistence equations and the coordinate domain itself has dimension $n=C+1=C-P+2$. In a specified reactive variant with $R\\ge1$, let $\\nu^1,\\ldots,\\nu^R\\in\\mathbb R^C$ be linearly independent dimensionless stoichiometric vectors and add the $R$ affinity equations $A_r=\\sum_{i=1}^C\\nu_i^r\\mu_i^1=0$. If the combined equation map has an invertible $(q+R)\\times(q+R)$ derivative minor at a solution and $q+R\\le n$, its local zero set has dimension\n\n$$n-q-R=C-P+2-R.$$\n\nThese are conditional local dimensions under the displayed nonsingular-minor assumptions. No dimension formula follows at singular solutions, on composition boundaries, or from equation counting alone; no empirical or general physical phase rule is asserted.",
            "current_raw_sha256": "86b0e377075c53b33e6adbf89636f5b61259d2466a6e01861b9ddb5754aa31eb",
            "scope_only_not_proof_acceptance": true
          }
        },
