    "ex-tdmath-vertical-separation-biconjugate": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-tdmath-extended-convex-function",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Fix an integer $d\\ge1$. Write $\\overline{\\mathbb R}=\\mathbb R\\cup\\{+\\infty\\}$ with its usual order and extended addition; in a convex combination set $0\\cdot(+\\infty)=0$ and $\\lambda\\cdot(+\\infty)=+\\infty$ for $\\lambda>0$. A function $f:\\mathbb R^d\\to\\overline{\\mathbb R}$ is **proper** when it never takes the value $-\\infty$ and its effective domain\n\n$$\\operatorname{dom}f:=\\{x\\in\\mathbb R^d:f(x)<+\\infty\\}$$\n\nis nonempty. It is **convex** when\n\n$$f((1-t)x+ty)\\le (1-t)f(x)+tf(y)\\qquad(x,y\\in\\mathbb R^d,\\ t\\in[0,1]).$$\n\nIt is **lower semicontinuous** when each weak sublevel set $\\{x:f(x)\\le a\\}$ is closed in the Euclidean topology, for every $a\\in\\mathbb R$. Its **epigraph** is\n\n$$\\operatorname{epi}f:=\\{(x,r)\\in\\mathbb R^d\\times\\mathbb R:f(x)\\le r\\}.$$\n\nOn this page “closed proper convex” means proper, convex, and lower semicontinuous in these conventions. The pairing of $p,x\\in\\mathbb R^d$ is the Euclidean inner product $\\langle p,x\\rangle$ of [[def-euclidean-inner-product]].",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-tdmath-fenchel-transform-and-subgradient",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $f:\\mathbb R^d\\to\\overline{\\mathbb R}$ be proper in the sense of [[def-tdmath-extended-convex-function]]. Its **Fenchel conjugate** is $f^*:\\mathbb R^d\\to\\overline{\\mathbb R}$,\n\n$$f^*(p):=\\sup_{x\\in\\operatorname{dom}f}\\bigl(\\langle p,x\\rangle-f(x)\\bigr).$$\n\nThe supremum is taken in $\\overline{\\mathbb R}$; its defining set is nonempty and consists of real numbers, so $f^*(p)\\in\\mathbb R\\cup\\{+\\infty\\}$. The **biconjugate** is an extended-real expression that may also take $-\\infty$ when its defining supremum is over the empty set:\n\n$$f^{**}(x):=\\sup_{\\{p\\in\\mathbb R^d:f^*(p)<+\\infty\\}}\\bigl(\\langle p,x\\rangle-f^*(p)\\bigr),$$\n\nwith the convention $\\sup\\varnothing=-\\infty$. For $x\\in\\operatorname{dom}f$, the **subdifferential** is\n\n$$\\partial f(x):=\\{p\\in\\mathbb R^d:f(y)\\ge f(x)+\\langle p,y-x\\rangle\\text{ for every }y\\in\\mathbb R^d\\};$$\n\nset $\\partial f(x)=\\varnothing$ for $x\\notin\\operatorname{dom}f$. A vector in $\\partial f(x)$ is a subgradient at $x$. All pairings use [[def-euclidean-inner-product]].",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-tdmath-extended-convex-epigraph-equivalence",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $d\\ge1$ and let $f:\\mathbb R^d\\to\\overline{\\mathbb R}$ use the conventions of [[def-tdmath-extended-convex-function]]. Then $f$ is convex if and only if $\\operatorname{epi}f$ is a convex subset of $\\mathbb R^{d+1}$, and $f$ is lower semicontinuous if and only if $\\operatorname{epi}f$ is closed.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L1",
          "source": "lem-tdmath-closed-convex-separation",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $n\\ge1$, let $E\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ be nonempty, closed, and convex, and let $z\\notin E$. Then there are a nonzero $u\\in\\mathbb R^n$ and a real $c$ such that\n\n$$\\langle u,e\\rangle\\le c<\\langle u,z\\rangle\\qquad(e\\in E).$$\n\nThus an affine hyperplane strictly separates the point from the entire set.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L2",
          "source": "thm-tdmath-finite-dimensional-biconjugation",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $d\\ge1$ and let $f:\\mathbb R^d\\to\\overline{\\mathbb R}$ be closed, proper, and convex in the sense of [[def-tdmath-extended-convex-function]]. With the Fenchel conjugate and biconjugate of [[def-tdmath-fenchel-transform-and-subgradient]], one has\n\n$$f^{**}(x)=f(x)\\qquad(x\\in\\mathbb R^d).$$",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "The domain is $[0,\\infty)$, so it is nonempty and $f$ is proper. Its epigraph is $E=\\{(y,s):y\\ge0,\\ s\\ge0\\}$, a nonempty closed convex set; [F3] therefore makes $f$ closed and convex. The point $(-1,0)$ lies outside $E$. With $u=(-1,0)$ and $c=0$, every $(y,s)\\in E$ satisfies $\\langle u,(y,s)\\rangle=-y\\le0<1=\\langle u,(-1,0)\\rangle$, so this is the strict vertical separator; its height coefficient is zero.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F3",
            "L1",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "For $p\\le0$ and $y\\ge0$, $py\\le0$, with equality at $y=0$, so $f^*(p)=0$. For $p>0$, given any real $M$ choose $y>\\max\\{0,M/p\\}$; then $py>M$, so the defining supremum is $+\\infty$. Therefore $\\operatorname{dom}f^*=(-\\infty,0]$.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "given",
            "algebra",
            "choose"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "At $x=-1$, the biconjugate is $\\sup_{p\\le0}(-p-f^*(p))=\\sup_{p\\le0}(-p)=+\\infty$: for every real bound $M$, the admissible choice $p=-\\max\\{M,0\\}-1$ gives $-p>M$. Thus $f^{**}(-1)=f(-1)$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "step 1.2",
            "algebra",
            "choose"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "For each $k\\ge0$ and $y\\ge0$, $\\ell_k(y)=-ky\\le0=f(y)$, so $\\ell_k$ is an affine minorant. Its value at $-1$ is $k$, and the conjugate choice $p=-k$ has $f^*(-k)=0$, so the same family realizes arbitrarily large dual values at the point outside the domain. The conclusion is consistent with the full equality in [L2].",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "L2",
            "step 1.2",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
