        {
          "case": "iff-forward",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "Take $C=2$ components and $P=2$ phases on the open coordinate domain $U=(0,\\infty)^2\\times(0,1)^2\\subset\\mathbb R^4$. Boundary iff-forward: The example states no biconditional.",
          "case_scope": {
            "source_section": "Example",
            "source_excerpt": "Take $C=2$ components and $P=2$ phases on the open coordinate domain $U=(0,\\infty)^2\\times(0,1)^2\\subset\\mathbb R^4$. Set all four chemical-potential coordinate functions $\\mu_i^\\alpha$ identically equal to zero. The two coexistence equations then vanish identically, so the solution set is all of $U$ and has local dimension four. The regular formula would give $C-P+2=2$, but its nonsingular-minor hypothesis fails because the equation Jacobian is zero.",
