    "ex-tdmath-water-reaction-boundary-tangent": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "L1",
          "source": "lem-tdmath-stoichiometric-tangent-cone",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $s,r\\ge1$, let $\\nu\\in\\mathbb R^{s\\times r}$ be a stoichiometric matrix (column $k$ gives the signed species-amount change per unit extent of reaction $k$), and let $n^0\\in[0,\\infty)^s$ be initial species amounts. Extents $\\xi\\in\\mathbb R^r$ are measured in one fixed amount unit, so $n(\\xi)=n^0+\\nu\\xi$ has species amounts in that same unit; $\\nu$ is dimensionless. The feasible extent set is the polyhedron\n\n$$\\mathcal F=\\{\\xi\\in\\mathbb R^r:n^0+\\nu\\xi\\in[0,\\infty)^s\\}.$$\n\nAt $\\xi_*\\in\\mathcal F$, let $J=\\{j:n^0_j+(\\nu\\xi_*)_j=0\\}$ be the active species set. Define the Bouligand tangent cone $T_{\\mathcal F}(\\xi_*)$ to consist of all $h\\in\\mathbb R^r$ for which there are $t_k>0$ with $t_k\\to0$ and $h_k\\to h$ such that $\\xi_*+t_kh_k\\in\\mathcal F$. Then\n\n$$T_{\\mathcal F}(\\xi_*)=\\{h\\in\\mathbb R^r:(\\nu h)_j\\ge0\\text{ for every }j\\in J\\}.$$\n\nIf $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^r$ is open with $\\xi_*\\in U$, and $f:U\\to\\mathbb R$ is differentiable with a local minimum at $\\xi_*$ relative to $\\mathcal F\\cap U$, then $Df(\\xi_*)h\\ge0$ for every $h\\in T_{\\mathcal F}(\\xi_*)$. Conversely, if $U$ is convex and $f$ is convex and differentiable on $U$, this directional inequality for every $h\\in T_{\\mathcal F}(\\xi_*)$ implies $f(\\xi)\\ge f(\\xi_*)$ for every $\\xi\\in\\mathcal F\\cap U$. Suppose $V\\subseteq\\mathbb R^s$ is open, $n_*:=n^0+\\nu\\xi_*\\in V$, $\\Phi:V\\to\\mathbb R$ is totally differentiable at $n_*$, and $f(\\xi)=\\Phi(n^0+\\nu\\xi)$ near $\\xi_*$. For each $j$, let $e_j\\in\\mathbb R^s$ have coordinate $1$ in position $j$ and $0$ in every other position; define $\\mu_j:=D\\Phi(n_*)e_j$. Then\n\n$$Df(\\xi_*)h=\\sum_{j=1}^s\\mu_j(\\nu h)_j=(\\nu^{\\mathsf T}\\mu)\\cdot h.$$\n\nThe tangent-cone and optimality claims are mathematical deductions. They do not assert that a particular physical system obeys this model or is at equilibrium.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "The species-amount vector is $n(\\xi)=n^0+\\nu\\xi=(2-2\\xi,1-\\xi,2\\xi)^{\\mathsf T}$. Nonnegativity of its third component requires $\\xi\\ge0$, and nonnegativity of its first two components requires $\\xi\\le1$; hence the feasible set is exactly $[0,1]$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "The elemental count matrix is $D=\\begin{pmatrix}2&0&2\\\\0&2&1\\end{pmatrix}$, with first row counting hydrogen atoms and second row oxygen atoms per molecule. Direct multiplication gives $D\\nu=(-4+4,-2+2)^{\\mathsf T}=(0,0)^{\\mathsf T}$, so every change $n(\\xi)-n^0=\\nu\\xi$ preserves both atom totals.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "At $\\xi_*=0$, only the water component is zero. Its directional change is $(\\nu h)_3=2h$, so [L1] gives $T_{\\mathcal F}(0)=\\{h:2h\\ge0\\}=[0,\\infty)$. A negative direction is infeasible because it would make the water amount $2\\xi$ negative.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "L1",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra",
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "For $\\Phi(n)=n_3$, the induced function is $f(\\xi)=\\Phi(n(\\xi))=2\\xi$. Its derivative at $0$ is $Df(0)h=2h\\ge0$ for every feasible tangent $h\\ge0$, and $f(\\xi)\\ge f(0)$ for every $\\xi\\in[0,1]$. This verifies the boundary first-order condition and minimum directly for this chosen mathematical objective.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "L1",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra",
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The example has one reaction and three species with explicit initial amounts."
        },
        {
          "case": "zero",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Steps 1.1 and 2.1 evaluate the zero extent at the feasible boundary."
        },
        {
          "case": "one",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Step 1.1 uses one reaction extent."
        },
        {
